From b87a78cd886f29695c5dada45d7af0c71b68fad5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Janice <djanice1980@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 21:18:50 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] drm/amd/display: local HDMI FRL watchdog diagnostics and
 link re-enable on loss of lock

Local debugging patch for an intermittent dark HDMI output on DCN 3.5 (Strix
Halo, native HDMI FRL, LG C2). Not upstream-shaped: the state is in file statics,
adequate for one FRL link.

1. Log the sink SCDC state once per 200 ms poll (drm_dbg, silent unless
   drm.debug=0x2): the DDC result of each read, the Update_0 byte, and the status
   flags with per-lane lock bits. Upstream ignores the DDC return value, so a sink
   that has stopped answering reads back as all-zero flags and looks happy.

2. Re-enable the link when the sink reports every lane unlocked while an FRL rate
   is active. Upstream reacts only to FLT_update, which the sink raises during
   training, so a link that trains, is acknowledged with FRL_START and then loses
   lock is never noticed. Measured on a dark screen: 1443 consecutive polls over
   five minutes, all status=0x40 (FLT_READY, no lane locked) with Update_0=0x43
   (STATUS_UPDATE set, FLT_UPDATE clear), while the source held 10G x4 and scanned
   out 10 bpc BT2020.

   The recovery is dc_link_dp_handle_link_loss(), the same pipe dpms off/on the DP
   HPD path uses, not dc_link_detect(DETECT_REASON_RETRAIN): with the sink
   unchanged, detect re-reads the EDID and leaves the link alone. Twelve
   detect-based attempts over 56 s produced no modeset and no lock.

   Debounced by three polls (~600 ms) so a modeset transient does not trigger it.
   Then one attempt every 5 s for twelve attempts (after a long standby this sink
   needs ~25 s before any attempt can land), and after that once a minute with no
   hard stop. It only runs while an FRL stream is active and the sink answers DDC
   and reports every lane unlocked, so an off or unplugged display never triggers
   it; the minute backoff bounds the cost if the sink is showing another input.
   A gap of more than 2 s between polls (output switched off) resets the episode,
   so a wake never inherits a half-finished episode and starts in the slow phase.

3. Record lock-state transitions, each re-enable request, the switch to the slow
   phase and the recovery at warning level, and promote the "200ms frl status
   polling starts/stops" messages to drm_info, so an ordinary journal tells the
   whole story: journalctl -k | grep "HDMI FRL"

4. Read the sink per-lane error counters (SCDC 0x50-0x5A: lanes 0-3 plus the
   Reed-Solomon correction count, each with a valid flag) while every lane is
   unlocked, and once more on the first locked poll. Logged at warning level with
   the time since the episode began: the first unlocked poll, then on any change
   at most once a second, then a final snapshot at lock. Never read on a healthy
   link. Purpose: find out whether the receiver shows life (valid flags, moving
   counts) before it locks, which would give a readiness signal to trigger the
   re-enable on instead of a fixed timer. (Answer, 2026-09-27: it does not; the
   valid flags stay clear until lock.)

5. The watchdog takes dc_lock with mutex_trylock(), never mutex_lock().
   amdgpu_dm_atomic_commit_tail() holds dc_lock while it calls
   cancel_delayed_work_sync() on this work item whenever a commit leaves no FRL
   stream (the output switching off). A blocking lock in the work deadlocks the
   two: measured 2026-09-27 20:33, a re-enable request raced the display going off
   and kwin_wayland, this work and dm_ism_delayed_work_func sat in D state until
   power-off. A commit holding the lock is changing the display anyway, so the
   pass is skipped (logged at warning level) and the retry schedule tries again.
   The same pattern exists upstream around dc_link_detect(); it was unreachable
   in 7.2.x only because the watchdog never ran (see patch 0002).

6. The watchdog no longer acts on the sink's update flags without the lock.
   Upstream's poll handled FRL_START, FLT_update and Source_Test_Update in place:
   it cleared them in the sink, reprogrammed the training pattern and the
   transmitter FFE and, on FLT_update, re-ran link training, all from the polling
   work with no lock held. Link training in a commit polls the same Update_0 flags
   every 2 ms (LTS:3, LTS:P) and clears them itself, and a 10G x4 training takes
   ~225 ms against a 200 ms poll period, so a poll can land inside a commit's
   training and consume the FLT_update or FRL_START that training is waiting for,
   or reprogram the transmitter underneath it. Unreachable upstream only because
   the watchdog never ran.

   Now each of the three flags is logged at warning level when the sink raises it
   (with the LTP request, Source_Test_Req and status bytes) and when it clears
   (with how long it was up). FRL_START and Source_Test_Update get no action.
   A FLT_update still raised on two consecutive polls, where training services one
   within 2 ms, has nobody servicing it: the poll requests the full link re-enable
   of point 2, which runs under the trylock of point 5 and re-trains from LTS:L,
   clearing the flag. Every 5 s for three requests, then once a minute while it
   stays raised; each request is logged with its reason, so every re-enable in
   the journal says whether it was loss of lock or an unserviced FLT_update.

7. Settle window after link training. The loss-of-lock debounce used to start
   counting at the first unlocked poll, and during a modeset that poll falls inside
   the commit's own link training, when the sink is unlocked by definition. Measured
   2026-09-28 across three 4K60 -> 4K120 switches: the watchdog re-enabled the
   link 21, 69 and 117 ms after the commit's FRL_START, while the sink needs 0.42-
   0.83 s after FRL_START to lock, so the commit's link never had the chance to.
   Every training start and the end of LTS:P now stamp frl_last_training_ts, and
   unlocked polls within FRL_SETTLE_NS (2 s) of it do not count toward the
   debounce. On lock the watchdog logs how long after the last training the sink
   locked, the latency this window has to cover.
---
 .../gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c |  37 +-
 .../display/dc/link/protocols/link_hdmi_frl.c | 454 ++++++++++++++----
 2 files changed, 381 insertions(+), 110 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c
index 83f6528..270129d 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c
@@ -2029,8 +2029,37 @@ static void hdmi_frl_status_polling_work(struct work_struct *work)
 
 		link_update = dc_link_frl_poll_status_flag(dc_link);
 		if (link_update) {
-			mutex_lock(&dm->dc_lock);
-			dc_link_detect(dc_link, DETECT_REASON_RETRAIN);
+			/*
+			 * trylock, never lock. amdgpu_dm_atomic_commit_tail() holds dc_lock
+			 * while it calls cancel_delayed_work_sync() on this very work item
+			 * (whenever a commit leaves no FRL stream, e.g. the output switching
+			 * off). If this work blocks on dc_lock at that moment, the commit waits
+			 * for the work and the work waits for the commit's lock: deadlock.
+			 * Measured 2026-09-27 20:33 -- a re-enable request raced the display
+			 * going off; kwin_wayland (in commit_tail -> cancel_delayed_work_sync),
+			 * this work (in __mutex_lock) and dm_ism_delayed_work_func all sat in
+			 * D state and the desktop stayed frozen until power-off. A commit that
+			 * holds the lock is changing the display anyway, so skipping this pass
+			 * is correct; the retry schedule tries again.
+			 */
+			if (!mutex_trylock(&dm->dc_lock)) {
+				drm_warn(adev_to_drm(dm->adev),
+					 "HDMI FRL: display commit in progress -- link re-enable skipped this pass\n");
+				continue;
+			}
+			/*
+			 * Full link re-enable, the path the DP HPD handler uses for link
+			 * loss: it disables and re-enables every master pipe on the link,
+			 * which re-runs FRL link training. dc_link_detect() with
+			 * DETECT_REASON_RETRAIN does not -- with the sink unchanged it
+			 * re-reads the EDID and leaves the link alone. Measured 2026-09-22
+			 * 08:36: twelve detect-based requests over 56 s produced no modeset
+			 * and no lock; the picture returned only when the sink latched on by
+			 * itself. Despite its name the function is generic (link_set_dpms_off
+			 * then _on over the link's pipes); its only DP-specific block is
+			 * guarded by skip_fallback_on_link_loss, which is false here.
+			 */
+			dc_link_dp_handle_link_loss(dc_link);
 			mutex_unlock(&dm->dc_lock);
 		}
 	}
@@ -11302,10 +11331,10 @@ static void amdgpu_dm_commit_streams(struct drm_atomic_commit *state,
 		if (queue_delayed_work(dm->hdmi_frl_status_polling_wq,
 				       &dm->hdmi_frl_status_polling_work,
 				       msecs_to_jiffies(dm->hdmi_frl_status_polling_delay_ms)))
-			drm_dbg_kms(dev, "200ms frl status polling starts ...\n");
+			drm_info(dev, "200ms frl status polling starts ...\n");
 	} else {
 		if (cancel_delayed_work_sync(&dm->hdmi_frl_status_polling_work))
-			drm_dbg_kms(dev, "200ms frl status polling stops ...\n");
+			drm_info(dev, "200ms frl status polling stops ...\n");
 	}
 	/* Allow idle optimization when vblank count is 0 for display off */
 	if ((dm->active_vblank_irq_count == 0) && amdgpu_dm_is_headless(dm->adev))
